@Article(time: 5) {
  @Intro(title: "Clients") {
    Rivulet speaks three client APIs, so existing feed reader apps can sync with it.

    The Google Reader API, at `/accounts/ClientLogin` and `/reader/api/0/`:

    - `POST /accounts/ClientLogin`
    - `GET /reader/api/0/token`
    - `GET /reader/api/0/user-info`
    - `GET /reader/api/0/tag/list`
    - `GET /reader/api/0/subscription/list`
    - `GET /reader/api/0/stream/items/ids`
    - `POST /reader/api/0/edit-tag`
    - `POST /reader/api/0/rename-tag`
    - `POST /reader/api/0/disable-tag`
    - `POST /reader/api/0/mark-all-as-read`
    - `POST /reader/api/0/subscription/edit`
    - `POST /reader/api/0/subscription/quickadd`
    - `POST /reader/api/0/stream/items/contents`

    The Fever API at `/fever` (`GET`, `POST` and `HEAD`); the query parameters decide what it
    answers.

    The subset of the Miniflux REST API at `/v1` that Capy Reader uses: the account (`/v1/me`),
    categories, feeds, entries and the integrations status.

    Logging in: the Google Reader and Fever APIs use the logins stored with
    `rivulet integration-credentials`, not the account password. The REST API uses the account
    password with basic auth, or an API key from `rivulet create-api-key` in the `X-Auth-Token`
    header.

    Apps checked against Rivulet:

    - NetNewsWire
    - Capy Reader
    - Reeder Classic
    - FeedFlow
    - NewsFlash
  }
}
